CROSS-CASE SYNTHESIS
==============================================================================================================
n=14 selected tournament rows:
  k1      cyclic-or-SCC rows 0/23
  k2      cyclic-or-SCC rows 0/23
  deficit cyclic-or-SCC rows 0/23
n=18 selected tournament rows:
  k1      cyclic-or-SCC rows 0/23
  k2      cyclic-or-SCC rows 0/23
  deficit cyclic-or-SCC rows 0/23

No selected row produced a strict pressure SCC or directed 3-cycle.

Working conclusion
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. The n=14 seven/gate ladders remain pressure-peelable even after the
   k=2 and threshold-deficit tournament lifts.
2. The n=18 lpd and gate ladders behave the same way in these exact samples:
   they increase endpoint debt and pairwise crowding, but not a mobile
   pressure core.
3. The best next proof move is therefore not to hunt arbitrary speed-level
   cycles.  It is to combine pressure leaves with labelled endpoint
   handoff leaves.  A counterexample must survive both peelings.
4. If future search finds a strict k=2 or deficit SCC for n=18 before n=14,
   that SCC is the first place to try THM-365-style arithmetic labels.
